                        "expr": "sum by (instance) (rate(leios_logmetrics_diffusion_eb_validation_reapplied_total{instance=~\"$instance\"}[${window}s])) / (sum by (instance) (rate(leios_logmetrics_diffusion_eb_validation_reapplied_total{instance=~\"$instance\"}[${window}s]) + rate(leios_logmetrics_diffusion_eb_validation_applied_total{instance=~\"$instance\"}[${window}s])) > 0)",
                        "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
                        "range": true
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "A"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "The same cache hit rate as the panel above, weighted by transaction rather than by validation. That one averages a ratio computed per validation, so every validation counts equally whatever its closure size; this one divides two counters, so every transaction counts equally.\n\nThey will not agree, and neither is wrong. A node validating many small cached closures and one large fresh one reads higher above. Weighting by validation answers how often a closure was cached; weighting by transaction answers what share of the work the cache spared. Which is the useful question is a call for whoever is tuning the cache.\n\nThis form is a Mimir query rather than a log scan, so it is roughly an order of magnitude cheaper and stays cheap as the fleet grows. What it cannot show is the per-validation spread: the 0 and 1 readings visible above are real, and that texture does not survive aggregation into counters.\n\nCounters come from leios_diffusion_process, so this panel is blank on any deployment without that module.",
          "id": 9,
          "links": [],
          "title": "Cache hit rate, counter-based (weighted by tx, not by validation)",
